#### Receiving events {#receiving_events}

The first thing we need to do is create some app code that will correctly receive the events.

Each event will trigger a request containing a JSON payload sent to your configured _Request URL_. The [Events API docs](https://docs.slack.dev/apis/events-api.md#receiving_events) contain a full description of the shape of this JSON, and the reference for [`app_mention`](https://docs.slack.dev/reference/events/app_mention.md) and the [`message.channels`](https://docs.slack.dev/reference/events/message.channels.md) contain any details specific to each event type.

Your app has to be able to receive and parse this JSON, then send an [immediate confirmation response](https://docs.slack.dev/apis/events-api.md#responding_to_events) to each event request as described [in the Events API docs](https://docs.slack.dev/apis/events-api.md#responding_to_events).

Here's how we might build our code for receiving events:

```
// Receive event payload to Request URL via HTTP POSTrouter.post("/", function(req, res, next) {    // Get event payload    let payload = req.body;    // Respond to this event with HTTP 200 status    res.sendStatus(200);}
```

Now that you've written code to handle an event, you can think about how to respond in a "bot-like" way.

