### Incoming Webhooks {#incoming-webhooks}

[Incoming Webhooks](https://docs.slack.dev/messaging/sending-messages-using-incoming-webhooks.md) are a way to post messages from apps into Slack. Creating an [Incoming Webhook](https://docs.slack.dev/messaging/sending-messages-using-incoming-webhooks.md) gives you a unique URL to which you send a JSON payload with the message text and some options.

Using [incoming webhooks](https://docs.slack.dev/messaging/sending-messages-using-incoming-webhooks.md) with Slack apps is very similar to the legacy process you're familiar with. Follow our [Getting Started guide](https://docs.slack.dev/messaging/sending-messages-using-incoming-webhooks.md#getting-started) to set up new webhooks for use with Slack apps.

### Updating and pushing views in modals {#new_methods}

Modals are containers that can hold a stack of one to three views. Each view is generated using [a payload](https://docs.slack.dev/reference/views.md) that defines a Block Kit layout, view functionality, and some state variables to track within the view.

Within an individual modal, views can be created and [pushed onto the stack](https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/views.push.md), or [updated while in the stack](https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/views.update.md). Complete [our guide to using modals](https://docs.slack.dev/surfaces/modals.md) to learn how to manipulate views and modals.

