### 1. Change your scopes {#adjust_scopes}

As part of the install process, your app defines a set of initial [permission scopes](https://docs.slack.dev/authentication/installing-with-oauth.md) to request from a user. Whether you're using the [Slack button](https://docs.slack.dev/legacy/legacy-slack-button.md#button-widget) to provide a link for users to install your app or your own [custom OAuth redirect](https://docs.slack.dev//authentication.md#step_1_-_sending_users_to_authorize_and_or_install), there will be a `scope` parameter that sets this initial list of permissions.

To generate incoming webhook URLs, make sure you include the [`incoming-webhook` permission](https://docs.slack.dev/reference/scopes/incoming-webhook.md) in that `scope` list. When you do, users will see an additional permission on the Authorize screen that allows them to pick the channel where incoming webhooks will post to, [as shown above](#create_a_webhook).

